{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Today they offered.</b> Rashi explains: what does it mean? That is, what answer did Aharon give with 'today they offered,' to Moshe who asked why they did not eat — as if because they did not offer the sin-offering they should not have eaten it? But all the services of that day were performed by Aharon, and even so it is written 'and you shall eat it as a commandment,' and it is written 'and the thigh of the terumah you shall eat,' etc. And Rashi further explained: Moshe said to them, perhaps you sprinkled its blood while onenim, etc. Our master Moshe thought that Aharon's status of onen did not disqualify on that day for any sin-offering, even for that of Rosh Chodesh. And what Rashi explained was brief; this is what it says: perhaps the blood of the Rosh Chodesh sin-offering was sprinkled by an onen, and you think that service performed by an onen today is invalid. Therefore Aharon answered him and said: 'and is it they' — who are laymen — 'who offered their sin-offering,' meaning the other sin-offerings? 'I' — who am the high priest — 'offered,' and I am able to offer them even as an onen, since they are consecrations of the occasion, and therefore they were eaten. [And 'ותקראנה' is from the root meaning occurrence and event.]" "<b>And if I had eaten of the sin-offering</b> of Rosh Chodesh, which is a consecration of the generations, <b>would it have been good in the eyes of YHWH?</b> If you heard a ruling for consecrations of the occasion, such as the goat of the eighth day and the goat of Nachshon, you have no grounds to be lenient with consecrations of the generations. And Rashi further explained: Aharon said to him, 'and is it they who offered,' who are laymen. And if you ask: but is it not established that they were high priests on that day, as we said above? One may answer: they were not made as high priests except to be stringent, such as for tearing, letting the hair grow loose, and leaving the sanctuary. But to be lenient, such as to offer while in onen-status, they were not as high priests on the day of their inauguration." "<b>Et their sin-offering.</b> Three sin-offerings." "<b>And if I had eaten of it.</b> The sons who were fit to assist me in eating have died." "<b>Like me.</b> I am forbidden in onen-status, but the Levites are not forbidden in onen-status to sing." "<b>And I ate the sin-offering today.</b> I ate from the sin-offerings that are eaten — as much as I was able, I and my two remaining sons." "<b>Would it have been good in the eyes of HaShem.</b> That they would come to the status of נותר. And if this sin-offering had not been burned, it would have come to the status of נותר, for we remained only three." "<b>Would it have been good in the eyes of HaShem.</b> This heh at the beginning is irregular — this heh in its pointing, for its rule is to be with hatef-patach."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The bracketed final sentence appears to be a parenthetical note on the linguistic derivation of ותקראנה from the root קרה (to happen/occur)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:11:24.622250246Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}